{
  "$comment": "P0-43: the one scope map. ci.yml's `changes` job and `pnpm gate` (tools/gate.mjs) both read it through tools/ci-scope.mjs, so a lane runs for the same changed files in CI and in the local gate. A lane runs when a changed path starts with one of its `paths` (a directory ending in `/`, or one file), or with one of `shared` (the inputs every language verifies: the contract, the shared packages, the CI workflow and its actions, the scope map and generator registry; a generator's or a ledger's own file is not shared, because its outputs land in the lane's directories and the always-on js jobs check the drift). A lane lists everything its job reads, not only its own tree: the Python lane names the samples and the two cross-SDK fixtures its tests open. A rename counts at its old and its new path. A push to `next` tracks green per lane: `jobs` maps each lane (and `js`, the always-on test jobs) to the display names of its ci.yml jobs (`*` matches any run of characters, as in a matrix name; `always` holds the jobs no lane owns). For each lane the changes job walks the ancestry of HEAD, newest first, over the CI workflow's push runs on `next`, and takes the newest commit on which every job of the lane concluded success, or on which the lane was skipped by a successful `changes` job (it was green at its own base then). That commit is the lane's base. A lane runs when a file changed since ITS base is one of its paths or a `shared` input, and always when it has no base; the full matrices apply. Output `js-base` is the js group's base when every file changed since it lies under `packages/` (turbo then tests only the affected packages), else empty (everything runs). `CI OK` on a narrowed run therefore means: every lane touched since its own base passed on this run and every other lane was green at its base; `ci-ok` counts a skipped lane as passed only on such a run (output `full` is \"false\"), which is sound only because a lane is skipped only when it was green at its base. On a push to main, a tag, a manual run, and whenever the ancestry, the API or a diff cannot be read, every lane runs and every matrix takes its full shape. `matrices` hold each matrix's two shapes: a pull request runs the floor and the latest, main and next run the full set. Edit this file, never a path filter in a workflow.",
